title: Falco rules, hands-on
guide: howto/falco-rules.html
promise: Learn Falco's YAML rule language from zero on a small CloudTrail lab, then turn the same language on a running container.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
It is for intermediate readers at ease with terminals, Docker, JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) and YAML, a plain-text format for settings. Falco, which judges events as they happen, can be new.

You need Docker and jq, but no cloud account.

== How the guide was checked
section: How to use this guide
Every command and rule ran with pinned versions in Docker Desktop on an Apple silicon Mac, and the outputs shown are what they printed.

Not tested: the amd64 images for Intel and AMD machines, and falcosidekick, an alert forwarder.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Read it in order. Paste BASH blocks into a terminal; save YAML blocks in ~/falco-lab, named by their first line. Unlabelled blocks show expected output. In steps 3 to 7, falco is a shell function standing for a longer command.

== Set up: Falco, falcoctl and the plugins
section: 1. Get Falco, falcoctl and the plugins
Images exist for Intel/AMD and Arm. falcoctl, the Falco project's tool for rules and plugins, installs the cloudtrail plugin (reads CloudTrail files), the json plugin and stock CloudTrail rules. Expect Falco version: 0.45.2, then Artifact successfully installed once per artifact.
```bash
mkdir -p ~/falco-lab/plugins ~/falco-lab/rules && cd ~/falco-lab
docker run --rm --name csoh-lab-falco falcosecurity/falco:0.45.2 falco --version

docker run --rm --name csoh-lab-falcoctl -u 0 -v "$PWD":/lab \
  falcosecurity/falcoctl:0.14.3 artifact install \
  cloudtrail:0.17.1 json:0.7.4 cloudtrail-rules:0.17.1 \
  --plugins-dir /lab/plugins --rulesfiles-dir /lab/rules
```

== Set up: the lab file and the falco function
section: 3. Point Falco at the file, and look at everything first
Step 2 saves the 24 lab events, adding the eventType field Falco needs. Step 3 saves falco-lab.yaml, pointing Falco at them, defines this function and saves everything.yaml, a control rule matching every event: falco -r everything.yaml reports Events detected: 24.
```bash
falco() {
  docker run --rm --name csoh-lab-falco -v "$PWD":/lab -w /lab \
    falcosecurity/falco:0.45.2 falco -c falco-lab.yaml --disable-source syscall "$@"
}
```

== What Falco is and where you meet it
section: Rules that judge each event as it happens
Falco alerts the moment an event matches a rule. By default events are Linux system calls, requests to the operating system; plugins add cloud audit logs. Platform and security engineers write rules, often starting from the Falco project's stock rules.

== The lab: 24 CloudTrail events and a leaked key
section: Walk-through
The lab is 24 events from CloudTrail, the record of calls made in an Amazon Web Services (AWS) account.

build-bot, a continuous integration user, has leaked its access key. Ordinary activity around it includes a sign-in without MFA (multi-factor authentication).

== What someone does with build-bot's key
section: Walk-through
From 203.0.113.50 someone with the key is mostly refused, then creates the user backup-svc, makes it an administrator with the AdministratorAccess managed policy, gives it an access key and uses it to stop CloudTrail logging.

== include: _cloudtrail-lab.deck

== A rule tests one event, then forgets it
section: How a rule works: one event at a time
This rule, only to read, tests each event alone, then forgets it, so no condition can count or correlate. ct.name is a field, a value from each event; in output, %ct.user prints its value. The source key names the stream.
```yaml
- rule: Trail stopped
  desc: Someone called StopLogging.
  condition: ct.name = "StopLogging"
  output: StopLogging by %ct.user from %ct.srcip
  priority: CRITICAL
  source: aws_cloudtrail
```

== D1: a list and a macro
section: 4. A list and a macro: trail tampering (D1)
Step 4's d1.yaml starts with these lines. A list is a named set of values. A macro is a named piece of condition; this one is true when the call did not fail. falco -r d1.yaml alerts once, for backup-svc.
```yaml
# d1.yaml: D1, CloudTrail logging stopped, deleted or changed
- list: trail_tampering_calls
  items: [StopLogging, DeleteTrail, UpdateTrail, PutEventSelectors]

- macro: call_succeeded
  condition: (not ct.error exists)

- rule: CloudTrail logging tampered with
  desc: A call that stops, deletes or changes a CloudTrail trail succeeded.
  condition: ct.name in (trail_tampering_calls) and call_succeeded
```

== Logins without MFA (D4): require success too
section: 5. Nested fields: console logins without MFA (D4)
In step 5's d4.yaml, this rule follows a naive one that checked only MFAUsed and also fired for alice's failed sign-in. Requiring Success leaves one alert, for bob. The json plugin's json.value[...] reads any value by its path.
```yaml
- rule: Console login without MFA
  desc: A console sign-in that succeeded without multi-factor authentication.
  condition: >
    ct.name = "ConsoleLogin"
    and json.value[/responseElements/ConsoleLogin] = "Success"
    and json.value[/additionalEventData/MFAUsed] = "No"
  output: Console login without MFA (user=%ct.user ip=%ct.srcip)
  priority: WARNING
  source: aws_cloudtrail
```

== D2: before you trust silence, run a control
section: 6. What Falco cannot do: count (D2) and pair (D3)
Falco cannot count, so the shell counts d2.yaml's alerts per address: with only AccessDenied listed, 203.0.113.50 has four, under five. This control, which must return something, finds the attacker's three Client.UnauthorizedOperation refusals. Listing AccessDenied, AccessDeniedException, Client.UnauthorizedOperation and UnauthorizedOperation makes seven.
```bash
falco -r everything.yaml -o json_output=true \
  | jq -Rr 'fromjson? | .output_fields["ct.error"]' | sort | uniq -c
```

== Stock rules: the first match wins
section: 7. Stock rules, shadowing, and tuning by override
Loaded after the stock CloudTrail rules, D1's rule is silent, without warning: by default an event fires only the first rule it matches. falcoctl replaces that file on update, so step 7's tuning.yaml, loaded after it, starts with this override.
```yaml
# tuning.yaml: change stock rules without editing their file
- rule: CloudTrail Logging Disabled    # d1.yaml covers this and more
  enabled: false
  override:
    enabled: replace
```

== The same language on system calls
section: 8. The same language on system calls
Step 8 saves shadow.yaml and starts Falco privileged, able to do almost everything the host can, so use a machine you own. It worked on Docker Desktop 4.97.0 for Apple silicon; if yours fails, use Linux. Only reading /etc/shadow alerts.
```yaml
# shadow.yaml: a system-call rule; with no source key, the source is syscall
- rule: Shadow file read in a container
  desc: A process in a container opened /etc/shadow, which holds password hashes, for reading.
  condition: >
    evt.type in (open, openat, openat2) and evt.is_open_read = true
    and fd.name = /etc/shadow and container.id != host
  output: /etc/shadow read in a container (command=%proc.cmdline)
  priority: WARNING
```

== Exercise: write D5
section: Hands-on exercises
Write D5: alert whenever the AdministratorAccess managed policy is attached to a user, showing who attached it, to whom and from where. Check that it fires once and nothing else does. The guide hides each answer under "Show the answer".

== Answer: one alert, and a field that misleads
section: Hands-on exercises
Saved as d5.yaml, it alerts once: build-bot attached the policy to backup-svc at 10:06:30. A rule on the plugin's iam.policy field never fires: it reads policyName, and AttachUserPolicy sends policyArn.
```yaml
# d5.yaml: D5, AdministratorAccess attached to a user
- rule: Administrator policy attached to a user
  desc: The AdministratorAccess managed policy was attached to an IAM user.
  condition: >
    ct.name = "AttachUserPolicy" and not ct.error exists
    and json.value[/requestParameters/policyArn] = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AdministratorAccess"
  output: Administrator policy attached (by=%ct.user to=%ct.request.username ip=%ct.srcip)
  priority: CRITICAL
  source: aws_cloudtrail
```

== Exercise: this rule fires three times
section: Hands-on exercises
This rule, saved as ex3.yaml, is meant to catch build-bot creating users or access keys, D3's two halves. The lab also has an administrator creating a service user. It fires three times. Which alert should not be there, and why?
```yaml
# ex3.yaml: build-bot creating users or keys
- rule: New users and keys by build-bot
  desc: CreateUser or CreateAccessKey calls made by the build-bot key.
  condition: ct.name = "CreateUser" or ct.name = "CreateAccessKey" and ct.user = "build-bot"
  output: "%ct.name by %ct.user for %ct.request.username"
  priority: WARNING
  source: aws_cloudtrail
```

== Answer: the and operator binds tighter than or
section: Hands-on exercises
alice's CreateUser at 09:02:40 is extra: the build-bot test covers only CreateAccessKey, so every CreateUser matches. With ct.name in (CreateUser, CreateAccessKey) and ct.user = "build-bot", two alerts remain, which a SIEM (security information and event management system) pairs for D3.

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- Records without eventType, or a rule shadowed by an earlier one, end silently with exit code 0: run a match-everything control first.
- Print a field before filtering on it.
- Counting and pairing events belong to whatever receives Falco's alerts.
